{
  "id": "s3-access-denied-kms-key",
  "title": "S3 PutObject AccessDenied after the assets bucket moved to a customer-managed KMS key",
  "description": "A push to main merges SEC-112: infra/storage.tf switches the assets bucket's default encryption from AES256 to aws:kms with a new customer-managed key (infra/kms.tf). The terraform job applies it successfully (its log shows sse_algorithm AES256 -> aws:kms and the new key). publish-assets then fails: every 'aws s3 sync' upload reports 'An error occurred (AccessDenied) when calling the PutObject operation: Access Denied', and the AWS permission-denied signature hint says the principal lacks the named action, which reads as s3:PutObject. But the deploy role's policy in infra/iam.tf already allows s3:PutObject, s3:GetObject and s3:DeleteObject on the bucket, and the same sync's deletes succeed: what the role lacks is permission to use the new key (kms:GenerateDataKey, plus kms:Decrypt for multipart uploads), which SSE-KMS requires on every PutObject. The key policy delegates to IAM (account root statement), so the fix is a KMS statement on aws_kms_key.assets in the deploy role's policy (or a grant in the key policy). Categories cloud_auth or configuration (IaC change). The keywords require naming KMS, the KMS permission or key policy, and the encryption change.",
  "repo": {
    "owner": "example-org",
    "repo": "storefront-web"
  },
